Java Methods:
A Java method is a collection of statements that are grouped together to perform an operation. When you call the System.out.println() method, for example, the system actually executes several statements in order to display a message on the console.
Creating a Java Method: To create a Java method, there should be an access modifier followed by the return type, method's name, and parameters list.

Access modifiers
•	public: Accessible from anywhere.
•	protected: Accessible within its package and by subclasses in other packages.
•	private: Accessible only within its own class.
•	default (no keyword): Accessible only within its own package.

Non-access modifiers
•	abstract: Indicates that a class or method is incomplete and must be extended or implemented.
•	final: Prevents a class from being subclassed, a method from being overridden, or a variable from being reassigned.
•	static: Associates a method or variable with the class itself, rather than with any specific instance of the class.
•	synchronized: A thread-safety modifier used for methods and blocks of code.
•	transient: Marks a variable as not being part of a serialized object.
•	volatile: Ensures that a variable's value is always read from and written to main memory, preventing potential issues with multi-threaded applications.

Abstract Method
A method without a body (no implementation).
Declared in abstract classes or interfaces.
Child classes must override it.

Default Method (Interface Method)
Introduced in Java 8.
Used inside interfaces.
Has a method body.
Useful to add new methods to interfaces without breaking existing implementations.

Static Method (Class-Level Method)
Belongs to the class, not to objects.
Can be called without creating an object.
Useful for utility or helper methods.

Final Method
Cannot be overridden by subclasses.
Used when you want to prevent modification of a method’s behavior.

Concrete Method
A normal method with a body.
Opposite of an abstract method.

Method Reference:
✅ Introduction
Method Reference is a feature introduced in Java 8 that provides a shorter,
cleaner, and more readable way to use lambda expressions when the lambda only
calls an existing method.

✅ Definition
A method reference is a compact way of referring to a method of a class or instance without executing it.
It is used when you want to pass a method as an argument to another method.
Method references work only with functional interfaces (interfaces with one abstract method), such as:
Runnable
Callable
Comparator
Predicate
Supplier
Function
etc.

📌 Why Use Method References?
Reduces code length
Improves readability
Avoids unnecessary lambda expressions
Keeps code expressive and clean

⭐ Types of Method References in Java
Java provides four types of method references:
1️⃣ Reference to a Static Method
Syntax: ClassName::staticMethodName
Example: Function<Integer, String> f = String::valueOf;
Equivalent lambda: Function<Integer, String> f = n -> String.valueOf(n);
Function<Integer, String> f = n -> String.valueOf(n);
2️⃣ Reference to an Instance Method of a Particular Object
Syntax: objectName::instanceMethodName
Example:
String name = "Rajesh";
Supplier<Integer> s = name::length;
Equivalent lambda: Supplier<Integer> s = () -> name.length();
3️⃣ Reference to an Instance Method of an Arbitrary Object of a Class
Used mostly in Streams.
Syntax: ClassName::instanceMethodName
Example: list.stream().map(String::toLowerCase);
Equivalent lambda: list.stream().map(s -> s.toLowerCase());
4️⃣ Reference to a Constructor
Syntax: ClassName::new
Example: Supplier<ArrayList> s = ArrayList::new;
Equivalent lambda: Supplier<ArrayList> s = () -> new ArrayList();


